    "text": "constraints defined above. We define the following robust optimal control problem: \n\n# **Robust Optimal Control Problem** \n\n\n\n\n\nUsing the reachable sets _{Xk}_<sup>_N_</sup> _k_ =0<sup>defined in (2), and the nominal trajectory in (18), it is possible to</sup> show that the previous problem is equivalent to the following problem: \n\n# **Reachability-Aware Optimal Control Problem** \n\n\n\nwhere _{Xk}_<sup>_N_</sup> _k_ =0<sup>dependonthechosensequenceofcontrols</sup><sup>**_u_**0:</sup><sup>_N−_1,andarecomputedfrom</sup><sup>_X_0.</sup> In this work, the reachable sets are approximated using either ( **randUP** ) or ( **robUP!** ), which yield convex hulls. However, we show next that computing the convex hull of the samples **_x_**<sup>_j_</sup> _k_<sup>isnot</sup> always necessary to reformulate common constraints found in robotic applications. \n\n**Sequential convex programming (SCP) and constraints reformulation** In this work, we leverage SCP to solve (20). The SCP technique consists of iteratively formulating convex approximations of (20), and solving each sub-problem using convex optimization. Specifically, at each iteration ( _i_ +1), the previous nominal solution ( **_µ_**<sup>_i_</sup> _,_ **_u_**<sup>_i_</sup> ) is used to linearize all constraints, and reformulate (20) as a quadratic program with linear constraints which can be efficiently solved using **OSQP** [50]. The solution of this problem, denoted as ( **_µ_**<sup>_i_+1</sup> _,_ **_u_**<sup>_i_+1</sup> ), should approach the solution of (20) at convergence, which can be assessed by evaluating _∥_ **_µ_**<sup>_i_+1</sup> _−_ **_µ_**<sup>_i_</sup> _∥_ + _∥_ **_u_**<sup>_i_+1</sup> _−_ **_u_**<sup>_i_</sup> _∥_ . In this work, we use the open-source SCP procedure presented in [8]<sup>5</sup> , which includes trust region constraints and additional parameter adaptation rules to encourage convergence to a locally optimal solution to the original non-convex problem. \n\nNext, we present simple methods to use sampling-based reachable sets to reformulate constraints. First, consider dimension-wise constraints of the form \n\n\n\nwhere _i ∈{_ 1 _,_ ... _, n}_ . For instance, such constraints can represent velocity bounds, and are incorporated within **_x_** _k ∈X_ free in (19c). To reformulate such constraints using the reachable set _Xk_ ( **_u_** ) and the nominal trajectory **_µ_** , it suffices to compute its outer-bounding rectangle ∆ _k_ ( **_u_** ), defined as ∆ _k_ = _{_ **_x_** _k | |_ **_x_** _k,i −_ **_µ_** _k,i| ≤ δk,i}_ . Using ( **randUP** ) or ( **robUP!** ), the bounds _δk,i_ = max _j |_ **_x_**<sup>_j_</sup> _k,i_<sup>_−_</sup><sup>**_µ_**</sup><sup>_k,i|_canbeefficientlycomputed.Withtheserectangularsets,(21)iscon-</sup> servatively reformulated as \n\n\n\nNext, linear constraints **a** _·_ **_x_** _k,_ 1: _s ≤ b_ on the _s_ first components of **_x_** _k_ , with **a** _∈_ R<sup>_s_</sup> _, b ∈_ R can be reformulated using outer-bounding ellipsoidal sets for the _s_ first components of **_x_** : _B_<sup>_s_</sup> ( **_µ_** _k,_ **_Q_** _k_ ) = � **_x_** _|_ ( **_x_** _−_ **_µ_** _k,_ 1: _s_ )<sup>_T_</sup> **_Q_**<sup>_−_</sup> _k_<sup>1(</sup><sup>**_x_**</sup><sup>_−_</sup><sup>**_µ_**</sup><sup>_k,_1:</sup><sup>_s_)</sup><sup>_≤_1</sup> �. Following derivations in Section C.1, **_Q_** _k_ can be computed as **_Q_** _k_ = _s ·_ diag( _δi_<sup>2</sup><sup>_,i_= 1</sup><sup>_,_...</sup><sup>_, s_), yielding an ellipsoidal set</sup><sup>_Bs_which outer-approximates the</sup> reachable set _Xk_ (projected onto its _s_ first dimensions). Using this ellipsoidal set, the linear constraint **a** _·_ **_x_** _k,_ 1: _s ≤ b_ is conservatively reformulated as **a**<sup>_T_</sup> **_µ_** _k,_ 1: _s_ + ( **a**<sup>_T_</sup> **_Q_** _k_ **a** )<sup>1</sup><sup>_/_2</sup> _≤ b_ , following similar derivations as in [8, 13]. Finally, this constraint is linearized, to yield a convex reformulation of the original constraints (19c), and obtain a convex quadratic program with linear constraints which can be solved using **OSQP** . For the spacecraft planning problem in Section 6, such linear constraints are obtained from non-convex obstacle avoidance constraints, by expressing them using the signed distance function, and linearizing the expression. This procedure is described in [8], where it is shown that it is conservative for general convex obstacles. \n\n> 5The implementation of [8] is available at `github.com/StanfordASL/ccscp` . \n\n16 \n\n"
